      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Pneumonia developing > 48 hours after hospital admission. Hospital-acquired
    pneumonia (HAP) occurs most commonly in critically ill patients and is usually
    caused by bacteria. Hospital-acquired pneumonia is predominantly caused by gram-negative\r\naerobic
    bacilli or S. aureus and is much more likely to be caused by a MDR isolate. Collectively,
    the non-lactose fermenting gram-negative bacilli P. aeruginosa and Acinetobacter
    spp. are the most common cause of HAP (about 25%\u2013 45%). Enteric gram-negative
    bacilli such as K. pneumoniae and E. coli are also common (13%\u201320%). S.
    aureus is also common (12%\u201321%) with approximately half of these isolates
    methicillin-resistant.\r\n"
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 3914de223fa8efc4787902589e5d6968, type: 2}
